{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,105p' AGENTS.md && sed -n '1,140p' docs/design/review/accounting-dossiers/compound-bond.md\"","aggregated_output":"# AccDSL project instructions\n\n## Mission entry point\n\nBefore substantive work in this project, read:\n\n1. [Mission control](MISSION_CONTROL.md).\n2. [Requirements and acceptance evidence](docs/plans/accounting-mission-requirements.md).\n3. [Current checkpoint](docs/plans/accounting-product-design-checkpoint.md).\n4. The gap records relevant to the task:\n   [initial audit](docs/design/review/gap-audit-2026-10-02.md) and\n   [expanded mission audit](docs/design/review/mission-gap-audit-2026-10-02.md).\n5. For the authorised master-program work, read the\n   [master checkpoint](docs/plans/accounting-master-checkpoint.md), executable\n   phase catalog and current `data/accounting-master/next-plan.md`. The older\n   corpus checkpoint remains historical evidence; current phase execution is\n   recorded by the master without rewriting frozen release hashes.\n\nThe mission preserves the user's goals across sessions. Current user directions\nand higher-priority instructions still control. Local documents must not be\nused to request fresh permission for work already authorised by the user.\n\n## Working rules\n\n- Identify the MC requirements addressed by each nontrivial plan or work unit.\n  Audit the plan before implementation, experiments or long diagnostics.\n- Preserve the full goals while staging execution. A small financing demo,\n  clean PDF build or six populated research ledgers cannot close requirements\n  for whole-company representation, research quality or implementation detail.\n- Distinguish direct user requirements, policy requirements and derived\n  engineering choices. Do not silently convert a convenient library, model\n  assumption or stage boundary into an immutable product requirement.\n- Keep source evidence, interpretations, assumptions, joint history families\n  and scenario-specific results separate and traceable. Preserve unknowns;\n  do not invent facts, probabilities, accounting compliance or completeness.\n- Close gaps with the specified evidence, and update the requirements register\n  and checkpoint after meaningful changes. Add newly discovered gaps rather\n  than treating the current list as exhaustive.\n- Follow the applicable policies under\n  `/home/chakwong/python/claudecodex/policies`. Preserve unrelated work and the\n  original paper collection. The existing `.git` marker is not a usable\n  repository; do not initialise or commit merely to create provenance.\n- Keep operational mission/checklist material out of the reader-facing\n  scholarly narrative unless it serves a real implementation-reference purpose.\n- Apply MC35, the user's internal-preparation-before-paid-review requirement.\n  Finish feasible source research, facts, calculations, software checks and\n  document repairs before referring a specific question to a professional.\n  Supply a proposed answer, exact sources, competing interpretation and checked\n  consequences. Keep unfinished internal work on the internal queue; “human\n  review pending” is an acceptance status, not a reason to stop that work.\n  Ordinary reader feedback need not be purchased from an accounting firm.\n  No professional engagement, outreach or fees are authorised by this policy.\n  See `docs/design/review/internal-before-expert.md` and the current checkpoint.\n\nThe current manuscript is specification-incomplete. Read the checkpoint and\nrequirements before resuming from an older note that says only final review\nremains. This file authorises no autonomous background campaign or external\npublication; it directs work undertaken under the user's active instructions.\n# Compound bond: issuer classification and settlement\n\nStatus: internally checked conditional illustration; actual instrument\nclassification is not established. MC17, MC19; gaps G4 and G7.\n\n## Facts and proposed answer\n\nThe issuer receives 100, owes two annual coupons of 5 and principal 100, and\nthe holder may convert that principal into ten ordinary shares. Currency is\nthe issuer's functional currency. Assume no fees, other options, contingencies\nor settlement alternatives; assume the share-conversion feature qualifies as\nequity. The market yield for otherwise comparable non-convertible debt is\nassumed to be 10%, not estimated from the example.\n\nUnder those conditions the proposed issuer split is liability\n`5/1.1 + 105/1.1² = 11050/121`, and residual equity `1050/121`.\nThe exact calculation keeps the components summing to 100. Using the stated\neffective-interest schedule, liability after coupons one and two is `1050/11`\nand 100. Interest expense totals `2260/121`, including discount accretion.\n\nIssue: debit cash 100; credit liability `11050/121` and conversion equity\n`1050/121`. Accrual debits interest expense and credits liability; each coupon\ndebits liability and credits cash 5. At maturity, redemption debits liability\nand credits cash 100. Conversion instead debits liability and credits issued\nshare equity 100; the original equity component remains in equity. The\n[checked journal report](../accounting-dossier-checks.json) preserves both\nsettlement paths. A 5% comparable yield gives liability 100 as an arithmetic\nboundary case; it is not evidence that a real conversion option has zero value.\n\n## Source and boundary\n\n[AASB 132](../../research/standards/aasb132.pdf), pp. 12–14 and 25–26:\nparagraph 22 describes fixed-number/fixed-amount settlement; 25–27 address\ncontingencies and settlement choices; 28–32 require the compound split and\nresidual; AG30 confines this treatment to the issuer, AG31 supplies comparable\nliability discounting, AG32 supplies no maturity-conversion gain/loss, and\nAG33–AG35 distinguish early repurchase and induced conversion. Paragraph 31\nrefers subsequent financial-liability measurement to AASB 9. This dossier's\naccretion calculation is a declared schedule; it does not fill the missing\nprimary measurement-source audit by implication.\n\nThe PDF's pp. 1 and 5 say the compilation applies to annual periods beginning\non/after 1 January 2023 and **before 1 July 2026**, incorporating amendments\nthrough 15 December 2022. Period start determines that condition; a calendar\n2026 annual period is inside it. The local source is an official Australian\nIAS-equivalent text with the comparison qualifications on p. 4, not proof of\nevery jurisdiction's currently adopted version.\n\n## Strongest counterargument and remaining internal work\n\nA variable share count, cash option, foreign-currency exercise amount, put,\nreset clause, contingent payment or related guarantee could defeat the assumed\nclassification. The arithmetic can remain perfectly balanced while the\nclassification is wrong. Do not apply this issuer example to the holder.\n\nFor an actual bond, first retain the complete contract and side agreements,\nprepare a clause-by-clause settlement table, establish reporting basis and\nperiod, acquire the applicable primary measurement authority, document the\ncomparable yield and test early settlement/modification where relevant.\nUS-GAAP and ASBE classification need their own dated sources. All are internal\npreparation tasks; do not pay for a reviewer to discover the missing clauses.\n\nA later review can ask whether named clauses X/Y change the proposed equity\nclassification under a cited paragraph, with both proposed treatments and\ntheir reconciled entries supplied. No actual clause requiring that judgment\nhas been supplied in the current synthetic example.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
